{"base":"ab423c1c075f7788e861b0f7dea7bad1fe6bd508","diff_base":"ab423c1c075f7788e861b0f7dea7bad1fe6bd508","head":"079c37eabd33cafd3e7925851ea3c95d76f6db44","previous_comment":"","repository":"Layr-Labs/d-inference","review":{"actual_usd":0.0,"bedrock_input_tokens":447550,"bedrock_output_tokens":9512,"bedrock_requests":9,"bedrock_unknown_usage":0,"changed_files":218,"covered_units":52,"depth_batches_pending":0,"errors":[],"findings":[{"detail":"The head text of configuration.md documents `DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE_MEMORY` (line 502), forwarded by LaunchAgent. It is an explicit opt-in for paged resident blocks and the recurrent RAM bank. Lines 531-532 add hybrid recurrent-checkpoint retention through `DARKBLOOM_CBV2_HYBRID_PREFIX_*`, and line 349 adds a MiMo complete-prefix gate. T-041's mitigations state that production never constructs or funds a resident-RAM prefix cache and that there is no production RAM tier or tier composition. T-041 also describes the SSD tier as default-on for every eligible CBv2 model, whereas line 501 says the default is on only for exact cohorts. A provider operator who sets the memory opt-in gets a shared-across-tenants resident cache. It carries the same TTFT hit/miss oracle (SEC-035, leak #1) with no TTL, no HMAC-keyed names and no at-rest encryption story. Those controls are only analysed for the SSD tier. I could not see the diff, so I cannot say whether this tier was introduced by this PR. Fix: update T-041 to cover the opt-in resident tier. State its salt scoping and lifetime, whether in-memory data is cleared on unload, and the exact default-on cohort. Or correct the docs if the tier is not reachable in production.","file":"docs/reference/configuration.md","line":502,"models":["anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5"],"severity":"low","side":"head","threat_ids":["A-001","ADV-002","T-041"],"title":"Threat model T-041 says no production RAM prefix tier exists, but the config reference documents an opt-in resident tier forwarded to launchd"}],"inte